## Prototypes
Under the hood, JavaScript/TypeScript inheritance uses **prototypes**. Every object has a hidden link to another object called its prototype.

When you access a property, the engine:
1. Checks the object itself
2. If not found, checks its prototype
3. If not found, checks the prototype's prototype
4. ...until it reaches `null`

```ts
class Animal {
    speak(): string {
        return "...";
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    speak(): string {
        return "Woof";
    }
}

const dog = new Dog();
console.log(dog.speak());  // "Woof" - found on Dog
```

When you call `dog.speak()`, the engine finds `speak` on `Dog` and stops. But if `Dog` didn't have `speak`, it would check `Animal` (the prototype).

## `instanceof`
Checks if an object's prototype chain includes a class:

```ts
const dog = new Dog();
console.log(dog instanceof Dog);    // true
console.log(dog instanceof Animal); // true
console.log(dog instanceof Object); // true
```

`dog` is a `Dog`, which is an `Animal`, which is an `Object`. The chain goes all the way up.

